title: The Last Route
start: start

== start ==
bg west_hall
music room_tone
The depot at ten to eleven is a yard of buses with their lights off and a hut
with a kettle in it. You have driven the 44 for nineteen years and you know
every stop on it the way you know the inside of your own house.
The route is being cut in March. Not the buses - the buses are fine - the
route, which is a different thing, and the difference is a meeting that
happened in a room you were not in.
Tonight is the last run before the timetable changes. Not the last ever.
Just the last one that goes where it goes.
-> the_pull_out

== the_pull_out ==
The first stop is the hospital and nobody is there, which is usual at eleven.
The second is the estate and two boys get on and sit at the back and say
nothing to each other for four stops, which is also usual.
You drive. You have done this so many times that the bus does it and you
come along, and the part of you that is driving is the part that does not
need you.
At the sixth stop a woman gets on with a bag of shopping and says the thing
she says every Thursday.
woman: You're late tonight.
you: Two minutes.
woman: You're never late.
-> the_seventh

== the_seventh ==
The seventh stop is a lay-by with a phone box and no houses, and it has been
on the timetable since 1974, and nobody has got on or off there in the
eleven years you have been counting.
You stop anyway. You always stop.
The doors open. The lay-by is empty and the phone box is dark and the wind
comes through the bus for the four seconds the doors are open, which is the
longest four seconds of the route.
-> the_question

== the_question ==
There is a man in the mirror. He has been there since the hospital and he is
sitting in the seat behind yours, which is the seat nobody sits in, and he is
looking out of the window at the lay-by.
You have not seen him get on. You would have seen him get on.
* Ask him where he is going. -> ask_him
* Close the doors and drive. -> drive_on
* Say nothing and wait. -> wait

== wait ==
You wait. The doors are open and the engine is running and the timetable says
you should have pulled away four seconds ago.
man: It used to be a farm.
you: What did?
man: The stop. There was a farm and a lane and a gate, and the gate is where
the phone box is.
-> the_answer

== ask_him ==
you: Where are you going?
man: Nowhere on this route.
you: Then you're on the wrong bus.
man: I'm on the right bus. It's the route that's wrong.
-> the_answer

== drive_on ==
You close the doors and you pull away, and you do not look in the mirror for
the next two stops, which is four minutes, and when you do look he is still
there.
-> the_answer

== the_answer ==
The route runs for another twenty minutes and stops at eleven more stops and
the man behind you does not move and does not speak again unless you speak
first.
At the terminus you switch the engine off and the lights stay on for the
thirty seconds they always stay on, and you sit in the seat and you do not
turn round, because turning round is a thing you can do once.
* Turn round. -> turn_round
* Open the doors and let the bus be empty. -> let_it_be

== turn_round ==
The seat behind yours is empty and has been empty for the whole run, and there
is a bus ticket on it that was not there when you got on.
It is a single, to the seventh stop, and it is punched with tonight's date.
You put it in your pocket. You are not going to tell anybody about it and you
are not going to explain why, and you are aware as you do it that this is the
first thing in nineteen years you have taken off a bus.
~ took_the_ticket true
-> the_morning

== let_it_be ==
You open the doors and you switch the lights off and you leave the bus to be
empty, which it is, and which it was, and which it will be at the depot where
somebody else checks it.
You walk to your car across a yard of buses with their lights off.
You do not look back at the seventh stop on the way home, because the seventh
stop is eight miles behind you and looking back is not a thing a driver does.
-> the_morning

== the_morning ==
bg west_hall
music fan_hum
In March the timetable changes and the 44 becomes the 44A and it goes along
the bypass instead, and the seventh stop comes off the map, which is not the
same as coming off the world.
On the first morning of the new timetable you drive the new route, and at
four minutes past eleven you are doing fifty on a road with no stops on it
at all.
* Say nothing to anybody, ever. -> END
* Tell the depot manager about the lay-by. -> tell_him [if took_the_ticket]

== tell_him ==
You tell him there was a stop on the old route that nobody used and that it
had been on the timetable for fifty years, and he says he knows, it came up
in the meeting, and that is why it is not on the new one.
You do not tell him about the man or the ticket. The ticket is in the drawer
in the kitchen at home and it says the seventh stop and tonight's date, and
the date on it is the last night the bus went there.
You keep it, which is a thing you have never done, and you drive the bypass,
which is a thing you will do every day until you stop.
-> END
